//! The BEHAVIOURAL half of the imprint: not what someone writes, but when and where they work.
//!
//! # Why this exists beside the linguistic half
//!
//! The linguistic dimensions need words. MEASURED on the maintainer's own twelve-word message: two of
//! the four discriminative dimensions came back ABSENT (no first-person pronoun, no habitual-misspelling
//! word), one was decisive, and one was actively MISLEADING — `apos_omit` read 0.659 against a profile
//! of 0.341 while an impostor's read 0.341 exactly, because at n=1 a single dropped apostrophe inverts
//! the dimension.
//!
//! These signals do not care what the message says. They are available on the FIRST message, whatever
//! it contains, and they are hard to imitate on purpose because an impostor would have to know the
//! person's hours, their project mix and their habits rather than their prose.
//!
//! # A distribution is not a rate, and the reading is different
//!
//! The linguistic dimensions are ratios: a profile value, a sample value, a distance. These are
//! CATEGORICAL DISTRIBUTIONS — a histogram over hours, weekdays, projects — and the useful reading of
//! one observation is not a distance but "how much of this person's history sits at this category".
//!
//! The sharpest form of that is ZERO. MEASURED across 2,283 messages: hours 03:00 and 04:00 hold
//! **nothing at all**. An observation there is at a point the profile has never once occupied, which is
//! a far stronger reading than a small delta on a rate — and it is available immediately.
//!
//! It is still not a verdict. `benefit-of-the-doubt-not-chances` applies here exactly: a person who
//! has never worked at 04:00 may work at 04:00 tonight. The reading states the mass and stops.
//!
//! # UTC, deliberately, and what that costs
//!
//! Hours are UTC. Local time needs a timezone database, and reading the local offset from a threaded
//! process is unsound in the crate that would provide it. For THIS purpose it costs nothing: the profile
//! and the comparison share one clock, so the distribution is the same shape merely shifted, and an
//! empty hour is empty either way.
//!
//! What it does cost is TRAVEL. A person who moves timezone keeps their rhythm and changes their UTC
//! hours, so every hour reading shifts at once. A DST boundary does the same by one hour. That is a
//! known and nameable failure, and it is the reason this dimension starts DESCRIPTIVE: it has to earn
//! discriminative status by measurement, not by being available.

pub(crate) use hour_of::hour_of;
pub(crate) use weekday_of::weekday_of;
pub(crate) use tally::tally;
pub use measure_behaviour::measure_behaviour;
pub use accumulate_behaviour::accumulate_behaviour;
pub use explain_behaviour::explain_behaviour;

use crate::Kind;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

/// One thing the person did: a message, when they sent it, and where they were working.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Event {
    pub text: String,
    /// Milliseconds since the epoch. Zero means UNKNOWN, and an event with an unknown time
    /// contributes to the project distribution and to nothing else — never to hour zero.
    pub at_ms: i64,
    /// Where the work happened. Empty means unknown, and contributes nothing.
    pub project: String,
}

/// A categorical distribution over one behavioural axis.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Dist {
    pub name: String,
    pub about: String,
    pub kind: Kind,
    /// category -> how many observations. A category ABSENT from this map has never been observed,
    /// which is the reading that carries the most information.
    pub counts: BTreeMap<String, u64>,
    pub n: u64,
}

impl Dist {
    pub fn measured(&self) -> bool {
        self.n > 0
    }
    /// The share of this person's history sitting at `category` — 0.0 when never observed.
    pub fn share(&self, category: &str) -> f64 {
        if self.n == 0 {
            return 0.0;
        }
        self.counts.get(category).copied().unwrap_or(0) as f64 / self.n as f64
    }
}

pub const HOUR_OF_DAY: &str = "hour_of_day";
pub const WEEKDAY: &str = "weekday";
pub const PROJECT: &str = "project";

/// The behavioural picture: several distributions, each over its own axis.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize, Default)]
pub struct Behaviour {
    pub dists: Vec<Dist>,
    pub events: u64,
}

impl Behaviour {
    pub fn get(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Dist> {
        self.dists.iter().find(|d| d.name == name)
    }
}

/// What one observation reads against a distribution.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct DistReading {
    pub name: String,
    pub about: String,
    /// The category this observation fell in, or `None` when it could not be determined.
    pub category: Option<String>,
    /// The profile's share of history at that category. `Some(0.0)` is the informative case: a place
    /// this person has been observed exactly never.
    pub share: Option<f64>,
    /// How many observations the profile has there.
    pub profile_count: u64,
    /// The profile's total observations on this axis — the denominator, so a reader can tell a
    /// share of zero out of 5 from a share of zero out of 5,000.
    pub profile_n: u64,
}
